## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical understanding of timer-driven thread wakeups, race condition handling, and efficient data structures in a kernel-like environment. It tests a candidate's ability to design a concrete mechanism that works correctly under contention while maintaining precise timing guarantees.

## Key Concepts

- Sleep queues and timer interrupt handling
- Min-heap data structure for efficient wake-time ordering
- Concurrency safety and time rechecking to mitigate timer jitter
- Correctness guarantees: ensuring no missed wakeups and exact timing

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal sketch of a per-CPU sleep queue
typedef struct SleepEntry {
  uint64_t wake_at;
  int tid;
  struct SleepEntry *next;
} SleepEntry;

// Enqueue thread for timed sleep
void sleep(int tid, uint64_t duration) {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  SleepEntry entry = {now + duration, tid};
  minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
  block_thread(tid);
}

// Timer interrupt handler
void timer_interrupt() {
  uint64_t now = get_time();
  while (!minheap_empty(&cpu->sleep_queue)) {
    SleepEntry entry = minheap_peek(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at > now) break;

    entry = minheap_pop(&cpu->sleep_queue);
    if (entry.wake_at <= now) {
      wake_thread(entry.tid);
    } else {
      minheap_insert(&cpu->sleep_queue, entry);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding safe concurrent data sharing is fundamental for OS-level IPC. This task tests practical use of atomics, memory ordering, and race testing.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffer basics, head/tail invariants
- Atomic operations: CAS, fetch_add, acquire/release
- Memory ordering and visibility on multi-core CPUs
- Overflow handling and backpressure

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t head;
  _Atomic uint32_t tail;
  int slots[256];
} ring_t;

bool enqueue(ring_t *r, int val){
  uint32_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h >= 256) return false; // full
  r->slots[t & 255] = val;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(ring_t *r, int *out){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return false; // empty
  *out = r->slots[h & 255];
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

